# Source comment hygiene

Code must be readable by someone who does not have the design documents open.
Comments that point at external artefacts therefore rot: documents get renumbered,
feature and bug IDs get archived, commits scroll away. When the pointer rots the
comment becomes noise, and the reader trusts it less than nothing.

## Rule

Source comments, log messages and test-case names must not reference:

- document section numbers (`§3.4`, `design §2.7.1`, `H8`, `layer 2`)
- design / feature / bug / slice IDs (`CORE-F43`, `BUILD-F02 S05`, `MAT-F08`,
  `BUG-EDITOR-008`, `S03`, `D6`)
- requirement or oracle labels (`I1`, `O1`, `O8`, `F11`, `Q3`)
- commit hashes

Instead, state the reason in place. Replace the pointer with the why:

```cpp
// Bad:  §H8 (ii): the checkbox binds the AUTHORED flag.
// Good: The checkbox binds the authored flag, so turning an ancestor off never
//       rewrites the author's choice.
```

Stable references are fine: in-repo file paths, type and function names,
constants, and the public API of the surrounding code.

Design, implementation and review documents are exempt - they legitimately
cross-reference each other and are versioned together with the IDs they use.

## When editing comments in bulk

Do not read a whole file, transform the text in memory and write it back.
An interrupted or mis-scoped transform truncates the file and the loss is silent
because the remainder still parses. Use in-place line replacement (`sed`-style, or
a patch applied to the exact line), then confirm the diff is a line-for-line
replacement (`git diff --numstat` should show equal additions and deletions for
comment-only edits).